---
/**
 * The privacy notice (spec Q.5), written for Josh as an individual
 * controller and for what the site actually does today. Update it whenever
 * that changes: analytics (Pass 9 or 10) must be added here before they go
 * live. Not legal advice; Josh checks it, and whether the ICO data
 * protection fee applies to him.
 */
import Section from '~/components/Section.astro';
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getProfile } from '~/lib/content';

const profile = await getProfile();
const { email } = profile.links;
const updated = '4 October 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy | Josh Lennon"
  description="What this site collects, why, who processes it, how long it is kept, and your rights."
>
  <Section labelledby="privacy-heading" width="reading">
    <h1 id="privacy-heading" class="text-4xl">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-graphite">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="prose-body mt-10">
      <p>
        This site is run by me, Josh Lennon, as an individual. I am the controller of the personal
        data described here. You can reach me about it at <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What I collect</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What you type into the contact form</strong> at /contact: your name, email
          address, your message, your answers to its questions, and, if you give them, your
          organisation and anything else you add. I also record when you gave consent.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A hashed form of your IP address</strong>, kept for at most an hour, to limit how
          many messages can be sent from one connection. The address itself is never stored.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing else.</strong> The site sets no cookies and runs no analytics or adverts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What stays in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        If you answer "What brings you here?" on the homepage, your answer is saved in your
        browser's local storage so the site can remember it on your next visit. It is never sent to
        me. "Change what brought you here", at the foot of every page, removes it. Answers you type
        into the contact form are kept in your browser's session storage until you send them or
        close the tab, so you don't lose them if you look around first.
      </p>

      <h2>Why, and on what basis</h2>
      <p>
        I use what you send through the contact form only to read and reply to your message. The
        lawful basis is your consent, which you give with the checkbox before sending. You can
        withdraw it at any time by emailing me; I will then delete your message.
      </p>

      <h2>Who processes it for me</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site, stores contact form messages, and runs
          Turnstile, the check that the form is being used by a person.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Make.com</strong> passes each message on to me automatically.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google</strong> holds the copy that reaches my email inbox and a private
          spreadsheet of messages.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Videos and music on this site load nothing from YouTube or Spotify until you press Play.
        After that, their own privacy policies apply to what they collect.
      </p>

      <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
      <p>
        Messages are deleted from the site's database automatically after 90 days. The copies in my
        inbox and spreadsheet are kept only as long as I need them to deal with your message, and
        I delete them if you ask.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under UK data protection law you can ask me for a copy of your data, to correct it, to
        delete it, to restrict or object to how I use it, or to have it in a portable form. Email
        me at <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> and I will reply within one month.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are unhappy with how I have handled your data, you can complain to the Information
        Commissioner's Office at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/"
          >ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
